      "outputTokens": 675,
      "costUsd": 0.0061245,
      "pronunciationText": "Last video ended on this pyramid of tins. There's one tin on top, then two, then three, down to fifteen on the bottom row. How many tins is that? Adding fifteen numbers one at a time is slow, and it's easy to slip. There's a famous trick that does it in one line. But a trick isn't much use unless you know why it works. So why does it work, and does it work for every arithmetic sequence?"
    },
    {
      "id": "scene-02",
      "changed": true,
      "audio": "public/audio/math-a12-f03/scene-02.wav",
      "durationInFrames": 1140,
      "worstWordGap": {
        "seconds": 0.9050000000000438,
        "after": "one.",
