      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 157,
      "outputTokens": 1082,
      "costUsd": 0.0098165,
      "pronunciationText": "Often you don't know the last term. But you do know it's a, plus n minus one lots of d. Swap that in for a sub n, and the bracket becomes two a, plus n minus one lots of d. So S sub n is also n over two, times two a plus n minus one lots of d. It's the same sum, written with a, d and n. Test it on the pyramid. With a and d both one, and n equal to fifteen, it's fifteen over two, times two plus fourteen. That's seven and a half times sixteen, which is one hundred and twenty again. Use the first form when you know the last term, and the second when you know the common difference."
    },
