      "pronunciationText": "Sometimes n is the unknown. How many terms of four, plus nine, plus fourteen, and so on, are needed for the sum to first go over five hundred? Here ay is four and d is five, so S sub n is n over two, times five n plus three. Set that above five hundred, and you get a quadratic inequality. Its positive root is about thirteen point eight five. But n must be a whole number, so the first one that works is fourteen. Check both sides. Thirteen terms make four hundred and forty-two, which isn't enough, and fourteen terms make five hundred and eleven. Because every term is positive, the sum only grows, so fourteen is the first."
    },
